[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) holds every report about a wrong date since April, merged as one known
issue with one fix queued. Read by tenant and by body, its reports describe three different bugs.
The queued parser fix closes only the first one.

<Callout kind="warn">
Not one issue. Three tickets, one per problem. Fixing the parser leaves the other two open.
</Callout>

## The problems

<ProblemTable ticket={4471} />

1. **`parser`: the shared parser reads dates month first.** Every tenant in #4471 writes the day
   first, so 7 April comes out as 4 July. Every tenant, every channel.
2. **`hint`: the booking-v3 form tells people to type month first.** It prints `MM/DD/YYYY`, then
   reads the field day first, the tenant's way. Only form reports from the three booking-v3 tenants.
   A correct parser does not fix a wrong hint.
3. **`shift`: dates move by one day.** Not a swap: Tuesday becomes Monday, one meeting lands on
   different days in Lisbon and Toronto, every booking in June is one day off. A time zone bug. The
   desk had marked these reports in `desk-notes.json` but still counted them under the parser.

## How this was counted

- One id, one report: the export pages overlap, so some reports come twice.
- The cause comes from the tenant (its locale and its booking form, `causes.ts`), not from the
  intake tag. Intake guessed from the reporter's browser, and the guess is often wrong.
- Counted the desk's way: each report under the cause its tenant gives it. A one-day move is also
  its own problem, so those reports count under `parser` and again under `shift`. Their ids come
  from the desk's notes and match the only report bodies that describe one.
- Decision [0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges on subject line. These reports use
  twelve subjects across all three problems, so the subject says nothing about the cause.
- Build with full locale data. Without it, no locale reads as day-first and every report is unplaced.

